              "passageTitle": "From Bitter Drink to Sweet Bar",
              "text": "Chocolate is one of the most popular foods in the world, but for most of its history it was not eaten at all. It was drunk. Chocolate comes from the seeds of the cacao tree, which grows in hot, wet regions near the equator. More than three thousand years ago, people in Central America were already growing cacao and making a drink from its seeds.\n\nFor the Maya and later the Aztecs, this drink was very different from modern hot chocolate. It was usually cold, bitter and often mixed with chilli pepper. It was so valuable that cacao seeds were used as money: with a handful of seeds, a person could buy a rabbit or a basket of fruit. Only rich and important people could drink chocolate regularly.\n\nIn the sixteenth century, Spanish explorers brought cacao back to Europe. The Spanish added sugar and honey to make the drink sweet, and it soon became fashionable among wealthy Europeans. Chocolate houses, similar to coffee shops, opened in London and other cities.\n\nSolid chocolate came much later. In 1847 a British company produced the first chocolate bar by mixing cocoa powder, sugar and melted cocoa butter. Then, in 1875, a Swiss chocolate maker, Daniel Peter, added milk and created milk chocolate. Factories made chocolate cheaper, and for the first time ordinary people could enjoy **it**.\n\nToday, most of the world's cacao is grown in West Africa, especially in Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana. Many farmers there are very poor and earn only a small part of the price we pay for a chocolate bar. For this reason, some companies now sell 'fair trade' chocolate, which guarantees farmers a better price for their beans.",
